@startuml
title UC-01 · Configure P2P Daemon

actor       User
participant "jmj -generate-config" as Gen
participant "Shell (redirect)"     as Shell
participant "Config File"          as Config
participant "jmj daemon"           as Daemon
participant Tracker

== Generation: no side effects, no privileges ==

User -> Gen : jmj -generate-config [-tracker ...] [-facade-addr ...] [-serving-addr ...] [-temp-dir ...] [-cache ...] [-repo-db ...]
activate Gen

Gen -> Gen : defaults + flags
note right : Flags fully determine the output.\nThe existing config is NOT read as a\nmerge base: the usual invocation is\n"> config.json" and the shell truncates\na redirect target BEFORE jmj starts, so\nthat file is already empty by now.\nSee the note at the foot of this diagram.

Gen -> Gen : validateFields()
note right : Values only — port range, address\nformat, tracker URL, paths non-empty,\nand facade_addr is a loopback address.\nNo filesystem access at all, so a config\ncan be generated on one machine for\nanother

alt invalid setting value
Gen --> User : stderr: "Invalid setting: <field>", exit 1
note right : Nothing on stdout, so a redirect\nleaves an empty file rather than\na corrupt config

else fields valid
Gen --> Shell : stdout: complete config JSON
deactivate Gen
note over Gen : jmj created nothing, moved nothing\nand wrote nothing. It never opens the\nconfig file for writing — there is no\nconfig writer in the codebase

Shell -> Config : write (">" or "| sudo tee")
note right : The SHELL performs the write, with\nwhatever privileges the user has.\nPermission handling, if any is needed,\nlives here and never in jmj
end

== Startup: validate against THIS machine ==

User -> Daemon : jmj [-config <path>]
activate Daemon

Daemon -> Config : readConfig()
activate Config

alt file readable
Config --> Daemon : settings
note right : Keys absent from the JSON take\ntheir default, not the zero value,\nso a config that simply predates a\nnew key keeps working

else file uses removed keys
Config --> Daemon : legacyKeyError
Daemon --> User : error naming the key and its replacement; refuse to start
note right : listen_addr became facade_addr plus\nserving_addr; buffer_dir became\ntemp_dir. Unknown keys are dropped\nsilently by the JSON decoder, so\nsaying nothing would start the daemon\non default ports with the user's\nsetting ignored. The file is NOT moved\naside — it is wrong, not corrupt, and\nstill holds settings its owner wants

else file missing
Config --> Daemon : notFound
note right : Not an error; defaults throughout

else file corrupted
Config --> Daemon : parseError
Daemon -> Config : moveTo("config.bak")  <<best effort>>
note right : Preserved for inspection, then\ntreated as missing. If the daemon\nhas no write permission here it logs\nand carries on with defaults — jmj\nnever REQUIRES write access to its\nconfig path
end
deactivate Config

Daemon -> Daemon : validate() against this machine
note right : temp_dir created if absent and\nprobed for writability (daemon-owned).\ncache_dir and repo_db_dir must ALREADY\nexist and are never created — the pkg\ncache and its repository catalogues are\nread-only to the daemon

alt environment invalid
Daemon --> User : error naming the offending path; refuse to start
note right : Config file untouched —\nthe daemon never writes it

else environment ok
Daemon -> Daemon : read repo_db_dir, load every repository catalogue
note right : Read-only. Supplies each package's\nexpected SHA-256 and exact size —\nthe only source of truth for\nverification. Refuses to start if\nnone can be read: without a catalogue\nthe facade could verify nothing and\nwould answer 404 to everything

Daemon -> Daemon : listen on facade_addr and serving_addr
note right : Two listeners, and they are not\ninterchangeable. facade_addr is\nloopback and serves pkg; serving_addr\nis public and serves peers. Only the\nserving port reaches the tracker

Daemon -> Daemon : watch cache_dir, scan for packages

Daemon -> Tracker : announce(servingPort, full list)
activate Tracker
note right of Tracker : Registration per liveness protocol\n(UC-05). Startup announces rather than\npings: a ping from an unregistered\ndaemon can only earn a 404. An empty\ncache announces an empty list, which\nis the deregistration path and exactly\nright for a daemon with nothing to serve
Tracker --> Daemon : 200 (registered)
deactivate Tracker

Daemon --> User : "Daemon configured and ready"
end

deactivate Daemon

legend right
  **Why no merge?**
  Reading the old config to overlay flags onto it cannot work for the
  primary idiom. `jmj -generate-config > config.json` has the shell
  truncate config.json to zero bytes before jmj is executed, so the
  "existing settings" are already gone. A guard that detects this and
  refuses to print is worse still: the file is empty either way, and
  defaults would at least have been a valid config.
  Flags fully determine the output instead, which makes redirecting
  onto an existing config well defined and idempotent.
endlegend
@enduml
